{
  "scene": {
    "id": "check-pilot3",
    "language": "So, when receiving the ball from a check, um, your teammate's going to have the ball maybe on the halfway line, maybe they're bringing the ball out of defense, um, and your job is to try and make an option for them and, um, sort of make decisions based on where the opponent tracking you is relative to your own body position. So, if I run into space, um, my opponent has three options, right? They can either follow me really tightly, follow me from a distance, or not follow me at all, um, and what I do with the ball when I receive it, um, should be dependent on those three options. So, if the opponent follows me very closely, um, very closely meaning, like, if I put my arm straight out, uh, I can touch him and I can feel him, um, then that means they're coming at the same pace that I am towards the ball, right? That means their momentum is sort of off balance. That can be taken advantage of if they're too close to me. So, if he's marking me too tightly and I've, can feel his momentum going a certain way, what I will do is, you know, shimmy one side and try to go the other side with the outside of my foot, or take it on the inside of my foot as well. Um, so I'm going to unpause here and maybe give us, uh, a chance to look at that. Um, let's see. [0]So, my teammate, run towards him, opponent[1] follows me, and my idea now[2] is to get it, fake one way, and go the other. And then suddenly, I have, you know, the whole rest of the pitch. Ideally, he's gone one way, and, um, been off balance by me going the other way, and now I have some time and space to, you know, possibly shoot, possibly find the pass, um,[3] there's the goal, and there we go. So, that's what happens if he follows me really, really tightly.[4]",
